      "id": 1,
      "text": "Story City earned its name from Supreme Court Justice Joseph Story, honoring the legal legacy that shaped early Iowa.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Story_City%2C_Iowa",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The railroad arrived in 1878, linking Story City to Ames and Des Moines and sparking its early growth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Story_City%2C_Iowa",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Story Theatre/Grand Opera House, opened in 1913, remains the Midwest’s oldest continuously operating theater on the National Register.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9,
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Story_City%2C_Iowa",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A 1913 hand‑carved wooden carousel, also on the National Register, welcomes visitors from May through September.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.traveliowa.com/cities/story-city-iowa/826/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Scandinavian heritage thrives in Story City, where Lutheran churches once held sermons in Swedish, Danish, and Norwegian.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Story_City%2C_Iowa",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "City Park’s historic swinging bridge, built in 1936 during the New Deal, still swings under the shadow of President Franklin D. Roosevelt.",
